+    "Executive Summary: Trim 25-35% of existing AMD exposure at or near $615.52, biased to the higher end given the extension and unresolved earnings-quality flags, and complete the trim on any push toward $640-660 rather than waiting for a reversal.",
+    "Goodwill/intangibles at 48.7% of assets means tangible book is only $26.1B — the downside asset support is far weaker than the headline P/B suggests.",
+    "He's right that the forward PE of 39.5x and the PEG of 0.57 look reasonable against 50% revenue growth and 1,400 basis points of gross margin expansion.",
+    "If Nvidia announces a competitive server CPU product, AMD's agentic-AI CPU premium compresses.",
+    "If Nvidia enters that layer, the premium AMD earns on that thesis compresses.",
+    "Metric: Forward PE; Value: 39.5x; Signal: Rich but growth-justified",
+    "Metric: PE (TTM); Value: 157.0x; Signal: Very rich",
+    "Metric: PEG; Value: 0.57; Signal: Attractive vs.",
+    "Prior lessons reinforce this: the 2026-07-24 Overweight (-6.4%, -7.3% alpha) taught that unresolved valuation gaps dominate favorable fundamentals in high-beta names ahead of binary events, arguing against any add; the 2026-08-25 Underweight (-1.9%, -2.4% alpha) taught that trimming ahead of a catalyst was directionally right but insufficiently aggressive, arguing for the higher end of the trim range; and the 2026-09-06 Overweight (-0.3%, +0.8% alpha) taught that when trailing multiples exceed 100x and the setup depends on hitting consensus EPS, size smaller and widen the band.",
+    "Reasoning: The bear won the exchanges that determine risk/reward at $615.52: the bull's PEG of 0.57 rests on a $15.57 forward EPS estimate requiring core earnings to roughly triple from a $4.40-4.60 run-rate, while the 135x core trailing multiple is dismissed as a \"lagging artifact\" — asymmetric rigor.",
+    "Signal: Rate sensitivity of AI valuations; Direction: Bearish; Source: News (24/7 Wall St.); Supporting evidence: \"Why Higher Rates Are Becoming NVIDIA's Biggest Threat\"",
+    "Signal: Retail valuation objection; Direction: Bearish; Source: StockTwits; Supporting evidence: \"@BigGoldmanSacks: We up 10% today ($100 billion) for no good reason as rates reach highest level in years and economy weakens\"",
+    "So the bull's \"cheap\" valuation is cheap only if you accept a consensus estimate that requires a 3x earnings expansion.",
+    "The bear says \"157x requires near-flawless execution.\" I say: the forward PE of 39.5x requires the growth that's already contracted and visible.",
+    "The bull says: \"Forward PE is 39.5x, PEG is 0.57, therefore cheap relative to growth.\"",
+    "The bull's own recommendation — \"scale in on weakness toward $534\" — is an admission that the current price is wrong.",
+    "The conservative analyst is anchoring on a backward-looking multiple while ignoring the forward earnings bridge that the entire bull case rests on.",
